[{"id":"VRCE-2-68-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":1,"english":"Having thus reproved his mother in great wrath, Bharata said again.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c0c310c7c636a02b4ad13eb41d9a54cc63ed0e7ff61f008bbd4512ff01e6c64d","compact":"211d70ab1321bc28b40652952cf858c8e12bf408374b9fca245ab86676c2e2ff","nasal":"211d70ab1321bc28b40652952cf858c8e12bf408374b9fca245ab86676c2e2ff"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":2,"english":"Do you bear yourself from this kingdom, O heartless and wicked Kaikeyī. Having been lost to righteousness, do not you lament me dead.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.871,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cc1150e73cb2051fbacbd453d17a4dd5229344aa94e1317281ebc6d0ca27f739","compact":"36f7127676a5c8a4700f24387364bf53b5bd7b3c4411a15536ef09949e8ac4ae","nasal":"36f7127676a5c8a4700f24387364bf53b5bd7b3c4411a15536ef09949e8ac4ae"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":3,"english":"What qualities of yours were taxed by the king or the exceedingly virtuous Rāma, that both of them simultaneously have come by death and exile respectively through your agency?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.806,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"708a0f5111c509cdc1237ff3beaa0ad07060ae67e6a38051d7c21183d64b26c2","compact":"0cbf3f59dbd8b96d7c189b0e442b287d72d8c3e84f2cde262ecaa63ae89857a6","nasal":"0cbf3f59dbd8b96d7c189b0e442b287d72d8c3e84f2cde262ecaa63ae89857a6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":4,"english":"O Kaikeyī, you have been guilty of homicide in consequence of the destruction of this race. Do you go to hell, never to the world attained by my father.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.672,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bbbaac0c8f0a12ccbb9da2f0c3172708d808575472eefc99e972939482f1dd04","compact":"3fa9726e6185781271da93c95f86d68870beb0ce464594ef831e4882c9270ad9","nasal":"3fa9726e6185781271da93c95f86d68870beb0ce464594ef831e4882c9270ad9"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":5,"english":"Since, renouncing Rāma dear to all, you have committed this sin by your grim act and brought me into fear.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d7bfe830eecd76cd109486ce1e02f9119ae831f7d2b533eaebd6a96bbb7a69ce","compact":"221498f60e852a8439c405b1cc61bec128b99d08482a630b87ceaaa40cfbe73b","nasal":"221498f60e852a8439c405b1cc61bec128b99d08482a630b87ceaaa40cfbe73b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":6,"english":"Through you has my father met with his end, and Rāma has taken refuge in the woods; and it is through you that I have come to ignominy among all creatures.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cb8249709adb539d083df3a52606bf9fe0fddc846f835b66fdada5af7e3d4b2a","compact":"87b0cea044611ad2e98d5162657ee9413266a31e784efcc20ce974abc6099fe8","nasal":"87b0cea044611ad2e98d5162657ee9413266a31e784efcc20ce974abc6099fe8"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":7,"english":"O you enemy of mine in the guise of a mother! O fell one! O you that covetest the kingdom! do you not speak to me, O wicked wretch!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bec0dac8ce9e6abd8e4d0020cdfc3edd27b5e3b865ce72f54ad78a420d15c9fd","compact":"356dbd902ab2235ebf93e41844f21c24feac28f09fcbcb44aedaeb1b44baeac4","nasal":"356dbd902ab2235ebf93e41844f21c24feac28f09fcbcb44aedaeb1b44baeac4"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":8,"english":"O slayer of your husband! it is because of you, stainer of your race, that Kausalyā, Sumiträ, and other mothers of mine have been overwhelmed with a mighty sorrow.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e697878f177a5117bac8a8ce4033119724ea2f87e51d97eecd191287e38bb93b","compact":"4085ae2ed06fdb6461745069f5f46ee8b8c4142117e12cff73494939b862fca1","nasal":"4085ae2ed06fdb6461745069f5f46ee8b8c4142117e12cff73494939b862fca1"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":9,"english":"You are never the daughter of the pious and intelligent Aśvapati, you are a Rākṣasī born in that race, you that have destroyed my father's race, inasmuch as the virtuous and heroic Rāma ever observing truth has been sent to the woods and my father has ascended heaven through your agency; inasmuch as you that have perpetrated this arch offence, have laid this sin on me, who have been forsaken by my sire, renounced by my brothers, and come by universal disfavour.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2838820b68e3cdbca9964aa8eea3aaba18b1fdfb285f4e429456917e8494d7de","compact":"671b6aee50d9a6717625a0e21ff8c96c7ffd6cca377159880d8d30494e0a924e","nasal":"671b6aee50d9a6717625a0e21ff8c96c7ffd6cca377159880d8d30494e0a924e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":10,"english":"You are never the daughter of the pious and intelligent Aśvapati, you are a Rākṣasī born in that race, you that have destroyed my father's race, inasmuch as the virtuous and heroic Rāma ever observing truth has been sent to the woods and my father has ascended heaven through your agency; inasmuch as you that have perpetrated this arch offence, have laid this sin on me, who have been forsaken by my sire, renounced by my brothers, and come by universal disfavour.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.712,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0e741fc9f6c4b2fcaed2af7a42b8368f071e6619ddd0c700c9ec96929fab2604","compact":"bf7bb2de046511413ff8c2a98604cbd7193115284528daf7f0971fc54fa5b835","nasal":"bf7bb2de046511413ff8c2a98604cbd7193115284528daf7f0971fc54fa5b835"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":11,"english":"You are never the daughter of the pious and intelligent Aśvapati, you are a Rākṣasī born in that race, you that have destroyed my father's race, inasmuch as the virtuous and heroic Rāma ever observing truth has been sent to the woods and my father has ascended heaven through your agency; inasmuch as you that have perpetrated this arch offence, have laid this sin on me, who have been forsaken by my sire, renounced by my brothers, and come by universal disfavour.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f98b251370e0caa23fa2030ca466a530ed76fb8ed47c36e0947cd7cce82d9b5e","compact":"9ab0bf9be9a15ad8b1dca40b101935d53394302d8358d01c9dfc9953d3dfa12b","nasal":"9ab0bf9be9a15ad8b1dca40b101935d53394302d8358d01c9dfc9953d3dfa12b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":12,"english":"O you of sinful ways, having separated the virtuous Kausalya (from her son), what world, O you that repairest to hell, shall be your portion?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.847,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"830e6d8f0154533578960dca7e38f5cb91991e657350cc7797817357f0615d87","compact":"7baa135da7d2a1fa676ed432cb9b3b642022121c157b2e22694ac8aeb152f8e1","nasal":"7baa135da7d2a1fa676ed432cb9b3b642022121c157b2e22694ac8aeb152f8e1"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":13,"english":"Does you not, O tortuous one, know that that one sprung from Kucalya's, self, my eldest brother Räma, who is like a father to me, has ever been the refuge of his friends?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.925,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c0f0dd70269db6069160f4f874b51845996b2350b5fd1bb39bae1bb7ab32596d","compact":"27908d85986d0ebf560345f88ba1acdead4ac0217684818de51dc3b9bffd4f5b","nasal":"d8dada3c0ee552f1dc64615b5bbc85cc68406a39c83ef9893f5cfa400ead505a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":14,"english":"A son born from all the limbs of his mother, comes out from her heart, and therefore it is that he is far dearer to his mother, albeit her friends are dear to her.* *Angapratyangaja: at., born from the limbs principal and minor. The commentator recounts the face, the breast, the abdomen, the hands and the feet as constituting the former; and eyes, fingers, etc., as the latter.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.676,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8f436560d0f0331aa4508960638fdbcb91c66d78cd9919dc522fd78cea594b04","compact":"dafe3ef9055b88c182e25f2a7ae7150e467f0a8f535671e58279289bf0e065e8","nasal":"2fd90101e6846ade42713af7e37b13c5f5cad5d8243657eb1391c3b459f8a47d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":15,"english":"Once on a time, it is related by persons cognizant of morality, Surabhi regarded by the celestials, beheld two of her sons toiling on earth and seeming to be deprived of their senses.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.724,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3fef8dcf3585706ea93b3fa1490a8d4b030709d6f5613f07df0dcf1ddd8e01a5","compact":"e54d3e307bb70115b04e4de3d1f4686b5ca5898d4c584519f1cca40e6c1000ff","nasal":"e54d3e307bb70115b04e4de3d1f4686b5ca5898d4c584519f1cca40e6c1000ff"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":16,"english":"And, when the day had been half spent, seeing her sons fatigued on earth, she stricken with grief for them, began to weep with tears flooding her eyes.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7c633f1fdce196c3f42d991aa7dd5b9093e65b88beb03f010ec749769044ea35","compact":"d3cf5afd9389a3eb9677365c1b9c05a1962530c7bd80e6947c37587196b00d1e","nasal":"5454eac1a6c1cc7cf80da2d3455ee6bd964f559ad53bbd3538db79cf9f9d097d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":17,"english":"And it came to pass that as the magnanimous sovereign of the celestials was passing below, her fine and fragrant tear drops fell on his person.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bb240796024867bcde3cfdfbef2a6b452792c877bcdfdbb715eab0e11f3f9a38","compact":"fc44887c0f5201ee7db5df4a7ab2e7a84acf2589edbb8099d810aceb387fdc87","nasal":"be925c2d2feb59159683ebd43248ec9536d4a6152f81f9bb87bed43e93638b39"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":18,"english":"Seeing that illustrious one burning in grief, the wielder of the thunder-bolt, Indra the lord of the celestials, waxing anxious, with joined hands said.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fa9e64e6384cf30b91870d80793a75e53620ce26b78570338df26a5981b96777","compact":"a664925d1c9897d2e1eb9d91f9a771797e7effe67f7a10e4fd69ef6c24e363c6","nasal":"88564d8e5b27d0f5555ebc25bcae1281a71ecaee00e0a21e8f30db7807d6c131"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":19,"english":"Is there any great danger in any quarter? Do you, O you that are intent on the welfare of all, say, whence is this grief of yours?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"351df1f6694603a4ae01bc16496ac4dfbed09d37fa1e1ce260a3883f658032ba","compact":"8d1797827fcf2689d9c5c64efcd04a9eca0c41931dee7dce7d0d56e452413767","nasal":"3f5ff53afb7a4bf41eae0c06b9d20e939756632ce03f28bb389821aca966a3e3"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":20,"english":"Thus addressed by the intelligent king of the celestials, that one well skilled in speech, the sedate Surabhi answered.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.873,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4a559dc97456d41bc14f1ff62aa8d9e99affa5c9e63f7ba91bc0c5077d298af3","compact":"db710afd5e144e23915cb8e87e5bdec0d99f9199df0f48c805e6430bb73b0674","nasal":"db710afd5e144e23915cb8e87e5bdec0d99f9199df0f48c805e6430bb73b0674"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":21,"english":"Auspiciousness, O lord of celestials ! No sin is yours. But I mourn my sons fallen into evil plight, having seen them lean, distressed, and burnt by the rays of the sun, Balivardas afflicted by the wicked-minded ploughman.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.746,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b072e07868d116526beeb4ee0e69a39818f4ea95d59b74513dc54b4f0dfb631e","compact":"741191c8e07bc2df1db4d24fbc382752e16d8ffc0bd4029fb36f03ee05414d82","nasal":"6850aa48b6a9393a7eb7a8d204487f5ea3f68333e08cf137903f573f62b3a0c6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":22,"english":"Auspiciousness, O lord of celestials ! No sin is yours. But I mourn my sons fallen into evil plight, having seen them lean, distressed, and burnt by the rays of the sun, Balivardas afflicted by the wicked-minded ploughman.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.41,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fd42d50ae63e5ac7b281375e7af5fafdb1135b8db1280486daa1eb095afff4b2","compact":"4a2ff9774046f1c5c7c7dd5dfce2e8dedb52feac14d175c313872518871aac7f","nasal":"4a2ff9774046f1c5c7c7dd5dfce2e8dedb52feac14d175c313872518871aac7f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":23,"english":"Seeing those that have been born of my body, cast down and in trouble, I grieve: there is none that is dear like to a son.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ba5ea7ac73925772083e2b1ecfaf51b6d152cbb6e686078b60062cd316fb2d09","compact":"8321d04bcf149f21b9d25877b8408e965b15cba879c58712e7e841655ef2593f","nasal":"8321d04bcf149f21b9d25877b8408e965b15cba879c58712e7e841655ef2593f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":24,"english":"Even that one yielding whatever is asked, auspicious, crowned with the choicest virtues,। although having all natural functions, showing equal kindness to all, and of unequalled character, who maintain all creatures, she who has a thousand sons, grieves (for her sons). How can then Kausalyā carry on existence without Rāma?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4529e3fde2bd143de7d475dba106fd2d15141501af252652769cdbf5d8019195","compact":"2376cae0696ece60e3c9eebbd79e6f42187e44baa35446c6a4d92284f5a8f76b","nasal":"2376cae0696ece60e3c9eebbd79e6f42187e44baa35446c6a4d92284f5a8f76b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":25,"english":"Chaste and having an only son, that lady has through you been separated from her son, (like a cow separated from her calf). For this, you will always have to suffer misery alike in this world and the next.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"95d33c2ed3678ded275de5592d10d1fd2bec3cecf3f01a07eb5af7800be558ab","compact":"feb55da40d268c5fd24432a58e66bd96c8ebdcf722de90d49878a904596fc082","nasal":"feb55da40d268c5fd24432a58e66bd96c8ebdcf722de90d49878a904596fc082"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-68-26","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":68,"verse":26,"english":"I shall for my part completely minister to my brother and father, and shall without doubt, increase my fame.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.898,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-74-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2068026","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4f941d9777354663e487802af8909b88b817d27f1db7174de64063959bfd4808","compact":"92f284a2cff03994ea43454f9b7058c